<section class="bhd-page__split"><div class="bhd-page__split-media"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/3763876/pexels-photo-3763876.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Athletes training together on an outdoor track" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><div class="bhd-page__split-copy"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">What a partnership can include</p><h2>Support that fits the setting</h2><p>Depending on the relationship, collaboration can include product support, event hydration, sampling, educational or social content, agreed sponsorship, appearances, licensed creative, and structured feedback from the people actually using the product.</p><p>The scope should be explicit. A gifted product is not the same as a paid endorsement, a team supply arrangement is not scientific validation, and an athlete's personal preference does not establish efficacy for everyone else.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Standards</p><h2>Authenticity needs boundaries</h2><p>Sponsored or gifted relationships should be disclosed. Image and content rights should be agreed before reuse. Health and performance statements should remain inside the evidence and the speaker's appropriate scope.</p><p>Best Hydrate does not ask athletes or teams to fabricate testimonials, guarantee outcomes, or imply that a race result, medal, selection or personal best was caused by the product.</p></article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout"><span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Working rule</span><h3>Real setting. Clear relationship. Careful claim.</h3><p>That creates a stronger long-term partnership than an exaggerated endorsement.</p></aside>
</section>

